\chapter{Bindings, shadowing, and scope}
\label{ch:9}
\index{Bindings, shadowing, and scope}

Use \OnxCodeTerm{let} for an immutable binding and \OnxCodeTerm{var} for local mutable state. A binding belongs to its lexical scope. An inner declaration can shadow a previous name, and a closure that already resolved the earlier binding keeps that lexical identity. Names inside a destructuring initializer are resolved before the new pattern introduces its leaves. Changing a spelling in one scope can change which binding a closure captures.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn main()
{
  let limit = 42;
  {
    let limit = 7;
    assert(limit == 7);
  }
  assert(limit == 42);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

A mutable loop counter is a local state location. A shadowed immutable name is a fresh binding. The checker maintains this distinction through type resolution and C name generation. It also constrains what may be captured and what may escape its scope. See \cref{ch:12} for immutable captures and \cref{ch:27} for the identity of a checked loan over a named place.

